# Gate policy (binding, Ed, 2026-10-27)

**Authority.** Ed, Claude Code session 015ASG1T, 2026-10-27. He answered "yes" to items 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 of the
pipeline audit's decisions (`docs/process_traces/2026-10-27-pipeline-audit/`), having said: "cut the agent review
rounds then - especially those that havent produced any meaningful catches - but learn from where they did, and keep
a minimal second set of eyes where possible, maybe 6.1 sol xhigh fast for speed sake". Directive issue #589 carries it.

This policy was made before any block-5, block-6 or BYTES result was seen. It changes how code and rules are checked,
never what is computed. Where a registration's own text requires more (the block-5 seal's §10, block-6 §13 and §17,
BYTES §19), the registration wins until it is amended under its own rule. It supersedes the review-round parts of
`docs/orchestration.md` and the D-184 four-model council policy.

## What the October record showed

- **Real defects came from executing reviews of number-bearing code, and from running things.** Examples: the #416
  NEG-8 reference-span blocker (RUN_STATE item 69); a reference silently dropped on a read failure (FIX-416 rounds);
  the twin diverging on compressed input (#573); machine tests and real-shaped data.
- **Late rounds** mostly found defects in the previous round's fixes, but some were real HIGHs, so a hard round cap is
  unsafe.
- **Councils on process questions, reviews of docs and bookkeeping, and serial re-reviews** added time and little
  protection.

## The rule

1. **Docs, records, RUN_STATE, briefs, bookkeeping:** CI only.
2. **Tests, tooling, orchestration, and collection code that cannot change a number:** CI and the change's own
   regression test. One reviewer only if the author asks.
3. **Code that can change, select or exclude a claim number** (reduction, calibration, harvest/exclusions,
   estimators, analysis, blinding, the recompute), and the agent census, stand-down and arm checks:
   - CI, a regression test, and a counterfactual showing the test fails without the change.
   - Two executing reviews from different model families, **launched together**, each told to probe and mutate:
     Sol 6.1 at xhigh, fast (`service_tier = "priority"`), and Opus 5.5. (Fable 5.1 replaces either seat if it is
     unavailable.)
   - Together they count as both the independent review and any cold pass a ruling asks for.
4. **Fix rounds:** both reviewers check only the fix diff, until a round finds nothing above LOW. If the same defect
   class appears twice, the lead writes a structural rule; a third time, consult Ed.
5. **Rule changes and registration text:** one executing refuter (Sol 6.1 xhigh fast) plus a recorded lead line. A
   judge rules only when a registration requires one, or when the refuter's executed result contradicts the lead on
   a counting or exclusion rule.
6. **Councils:** only for a genuine science-design fork that changes what is measured or claimed. One seat per family,
   one round, and the lead or Ed decides. Process questions get no council. Ed can still ask for one.
7. **Unchanged:** the #416 three-family audit before a claim-bearing run; the agent census and physical-hazard
   refusals; file hashes and custody manifests; battery float (#421); blinding; the independent recompute and its
   agreement bars; CI.
8. **Release:** no cold gate beyond what the registration names (RUN_STATE item 37(ii), 2026-10-14).
9. **Pruning tally:** each session record ends with a table: gate, ran, caught_number, caught_session_refusal,
   evidence. A gate with no catch in three sessions is proposed to Ed for removal.

## Amendment, 2026-10-27 evening: who decides, and three lightenings

**Ed's words:** "i want you, the one running, with actual logs and references, deciding sensible but minimal gates, not
me who's 99% hands off - reading your reports."

1. **Who decides.** The lead (the orchestrator, or the interactive lead session when one is running) picks the gate
   level for each change under the rules above, and records the choice and its reason in one line in the session
   record. Ed is told in reports. He is asked only about science-design forks, and about changes to what a
   registration computes, excludes or counts.
2. **Refuters on registration text.** One executing refuter only when the text changes what is computed, excluded,
   counted or printed in a registered result. An addendum that adds a separately labelled result and leaves the
   registered analysis byte-identical (for example the BYTES extension of 2026-10-27) needs CI and the lead's check
   only.
3. **Reviews block only what they cover.** A review of code that cannot change a number runs alongside work and blocks
   nothing. A pending review of extension packs never holds a registered session.
4. **The three-family audit (#416)** runs once per freeze of collection code that will produce claim data, not once per
   pack.

Unchanged: rule 3's two cross-family executing reviews for code that computes or selects a published number, the
independent recompute and comparator, registration before data, blinding, hashes, physical-hazard refusals, the agent
check during collection, and CI. October's record supports keeping them (rule "What the October record showed").

# Ed's ruling, 2026-10-27: L9 acceptance without full-size synthetic runs; the blind dry run is the full-size test

**Status: binding (Ed, owner).** Given in Claude Code cloud session 015ASG1T on 2026-10-27, Ed present.

**Ed's words.** Asked whether the full-size synthetic test runs were needed ("do we need to do all this or is this
excessive caution?"), Ed answered the proposal below: "yes do it ... just get l9 working again - i want to know the
full front to back results of block 5 and 6 asap". Directive issue #587 carries the scheduling side.

**What it amends.** The Build paragraph of the L9 ruling
(`docs/process_traces/2026-10-20-magistrate-4b350f47/L9-RULING.md`): "Then the executing review, the J1-J6 and C5-C9
reruns on the Mac with no conditional seams, and the cold Fable and Opus passes at L9's merge." It amends nothing
registered or sealed: the block-5 registration, analysis plan, flag catalog, estimators, exclusions, kept counts,
blinding, the independent recompute and its agreement bars, and the release record are unchanged.

## Why

- The blind dry run reads the real, immutable block-5 archives with every number closed. A tooling refusal there
  is cured blind and the run restarts in a fresh custody root (scout report
  `report/2026-10-22-blind-dry-run-scout:.codex-report/BLIND-DRY-RUN-SCOUT.md` §3). So it is already a safe
  full-size end-to-end test, on the actual data. A full-size synthetic run before it rehearses a rehearsal.
- The full-size synthetic module takes about 6 h per run on a 5-vCPU cloud VM (J6, two runs, about 13 h), so every
  fix round costs most of a day. L9 has not reached merge, and block 5 has waited, collected, since 2026-10-12.
- The real release already refuses when its outputs differ from the dry run's (`release_digest_differs_from_dry_run`),
  so the full-size double run of the release-equality test duplicates a check the release itself makes.

## The rule

1. **L9 merges** after: its changed code's tests and the J1-J6 / C5-C9 predicates pass on **reduced-size synthetic
   fixtures** that still drive every registered step (0 to 11, including 3b, 9b and the registered p42 exits) through
   the real programs; the six short modules pass (`tests.test_authentication_io`, `tests.test_docs_freshness`,
   `tests.b5_analysis.test_sensitivity_guard`, `tests.b5_analysis.test_mint_kept_units`,
   `tests.b5_analysis.test_s10_chain_defects`, `tests.test_r4_acceptance_oracle`); one independent executing review
   finds no blocker (or its blockers are fixed); CI is green. No assertion is weakened to get there; a reduced fixture
   changes sizes, not checks.
   **Amended the same day (lead, under Ed's instruction "the only real rule is good science at a speedy cadence").**
   Seat L9-2 showed reduced fixtures cannot keep every check: L9's own `kept_units.py` refuses
   (`exclusions_plan_mismatch`) unless each floor cell has its registered 10 repeats and 10 quads. It also measured
   that 63 % of the joined run's wall time is 82 refits of one calibration's bytes, because the existing
   caller-owned `physics_cache` (`joulewise/cli.py` `validate_bundle`, already used by `joulewise/b5/harvest.py`) is
   not shared across the dry-run path. So item 1 becomes:
   - the six short modules;
   - one shared `physics_cache` per driver run, keyed by the calibration artifact's content hash, with failures never
     cached. The wiring stays in non-governed code (`joulewise/whole_window.py`, `joulewise/cli.py`,
     `joulewise/b5_analysis/`), with a test that cached and uncached outputs are byte-identical;
   - one full-size `test_dry_run` run with the cache on;
   - one executing review; CI.
2. **The full-size end-to-end test is the real block-5 blind dry run on the Mac**, scheduled as directive #587 says.
3. **The cold Fable and Opus passes** on L9 move from "at merge" to "before the release event". They may run in
   parallel with the dry run. The release still needs them.
4. **Release equality** is tested on a reduced fixture; the real release's digest comparison is the full-size check.
5. **Full-size synthetic runs** of `test_dry_run`, `test_release_equality` and `test_chain_fixture` become optional
   diagnostics, not merge gates.
6. **Step 9b's calibration refit goes to a ruling before the real dry run.** The analysis replay refits the pulse
   calibration under the frozen 120 s host-safety wall deadline, whose firing the code marks non-reproducible; it
   fired in the full-size synthetic runs (JouleWise-data `codex/l9-joined`, report "Final diagnosis"). Options for
   the ruling: (a) the analysis replay keeps the refit and the cell budget but does not inherit the wall deadline;
   (b) the replay checks the harvest-recorded fit by hash binding and leaves arithmetic verification to the
   independent recompute; (c) keep it as is, with the reason it cannot make the release irreproducible.
   **Decided by Ed, 2026-10-27: (c)** ("yes, sure, record c as my ruling"). Reason: (a), and also the per-member
   refit cache seat L9-2 proposed, would edit `joulewise/powermetrics_fiducial.py` or `joulewise/reduce.py`. Those
   are governed estimator-source pins (`ESTIMATOR_CODE_PATHS`, `joulewise/calibration_bracketing.py`), and the last
   change to the first one forced a calibration-acceptance reissue (`_r2`). So for block 5's first dry run:
   - The wall deadline stays. The dry run runs on the quiet Mac, where the same calibration completed at harvest.
   - A deadline firing is treated as a tooling refusal: cured blind, rerun in a fresh custody root (scout report §3).
   - **Condition:** seat L9-2 confirms, with executed evidence, that a firing anywhere on the dry-run path stops
     with a refusal and cannot change a kept unit, a lost-reference list or a printed number. If it cannot confirm
     that, this choice returns to Ed before the dry run.
   - (a) and the refit cache follow later as one governed change with its reissue, unless the cache can live
     entirely in non-governed code with byte-identical outputs (then it is an ordinary L9 change).
   - **Status, same day.**
     - The cache landed in non-governed code (`lane/2026-10-27-l9-2` b5df45a): an ordinary L9 change.
     - The condition first failed. Step 2's `reduce` exited 0 on a forced firing, with 4 calibration-dependent
       summary keys changed.
     - Driver tripwire 96f1f1fe6 now refuses at step 2 (`rereduction_calibration_dependent_fields_differ`) when
       any of those keys differs from the kept bundle's stored summary. Later steps are not run.
       `tests/b5_analysis/test_deadline_tripwire.py` holds the forced-firing case, the mutation proof and byte
       identity of the unforced path.
     - The other refit sites already end in refusals (L9-2 report). The condition is met, subject to the executing
       review (session L9-REV-1).
     - Margin: one detection of the 59-pulse calibration took 10.29 s on an idle 4-vCPU VM against the 120 s
       deadline. The Mac is not yet measured.
6a. **The Mac goes to analysis first (Ed, 2026-10-27):** "bytes and that work can pause until this is done if you
   need the quiet mac for analysis". The block-5 blind dry run takes precedence over BYTES sessions, s1-p1 included.
   BYTES's registered end (no t0 after 2026-11-02 00:00 PDT) still binds. If the dry run would push s1-p1 past it,
   the lead tells Ed before holding the loop, so he can choose between the fit set and the date, or amend the end
   date prospectively while BYTES is still blinded.
6b. **Permission to move the block-5 dry run to the analysis laptop (Ed, 2026-10-27):** "yes you have my ok for both
   moving the block 5 dry run here, and yes, orchestrator should be allowed to package and upload the archives between
   sessions", then: "i was giving you permission not a mandate". This is a permission, **not active** until the
   conditions in `BLOCK5-TRANSFER.md` beside this file hold and that file ends with `B5-TRANSFER: GO`. If it becomes
   active, the dry run runs on Ed's MacBook Air from hash-verified compressed copies, and BYTES never needs to hold
   for it. Until then items 6 and 6a stand unchanged.
8. **Review stage amended (Ed, 2026-10-27, gate policy `docs/process/gate_policy.md` rule 3).** For L9, the
   "one independent executing review" of item 1 and the cold Fable and Opus passes of item 3 become one stage: two
   executing reviews launched together, Sol 6.1 xhigh fast and Opus 5.5, each told to probe and mutate. Fix rounds:
   both check only the fix diff until nothing above LOW remains. Item 4's reduced-fixture release test is infeasible
   (L9-2 report); release equality is checked by the real release-mode rerun.
9. **Ruling 6b stays on hold (Ed, 2026-10-27: "hold on 2").** The audit's critique showed plain iCloud tars break
   custody on the laptop (they land where any agent running as `edr` can read them), the laptop's disk was never
   sized, and iCloud uploads on the measurement Mac run on into sessions. Until a transfer design closes those, block
   5's dry run is on the measurement Mac at a BYTES session boundary (items 6 and 6a).
7. **Block 6** follows the same principle wherever its registration allows. Where the block-6 registration itself
   requires a synthetic stage chain before its blind run, that requirement stands until amended by a dated entry
   under its §17 rules.

